  "response": "정확히 뭘 이야기해야 할지 모르겠다고 해 주신 것 자체가, 지금 머릿속이 얼마나 복잡한지를 보여주는 것 같기도 해요.\n\n두 달 전부터 잠이 안 왔다는 건 꽤 오래된 일인데, 그 사이에 버텨오다가 여자친구 그 말 한마디가 계기가 되어서 더 무거워진 느낌이신 건가요. \"네가 무슨 생각을 하는지 모르겠다\"는 말이, 사실은 그 전부터 혼자 감당하고 있던 것들이 한꺼번에 올라오는 계기가 된 것처럼 느껴지기도 하고요.\n\n\"뭐가 다 귀찮아지는 느낌\"이라는 표현이 마음에 걸리는데요, 그 귀찮음이 예전에도 느꼈던 것과 같은 건지, 아니면 요즘 유난히 다르게 느껴지는 건지 궁금해요.",
  "reasoning": "The user is presenting a counseling dialogue. The client has just shared that they've been having trouble sleeping for about two months, it got worse a few days ago after their girlfriend said \"I don't know what you're thinking,\" and now they feel their mind is cluttered, mornings are hard, and everything feels tedious/unmotivating.\n\nI need to respond as a counselor. Let me think about what's appropriate here:\n\n1. The client has shared something vulnerable - sleep issues, relationship tension, loss of motivation\n2. I should acknowledge their experience without minimizing\n3. I should not jump to solutions or be overly reassuring\n4. I should validate what they've shared and perhaps gently explore further\n5. One question max, only if needed\n6. Natural, conversational Korean\n\nThe client seems to be in a state where:\n- There's a trigger (girlfriend's comment) that intensified existing sleep problems\n- They feel a general sense of apathy/anhedonia (\"귀찮아지는 느낌\")\n- They're unsure what to talk about, which suggests some confusion or overwhelm\n\nA good response would:\n- Acknowledge that it's okay to not know exactly what to talk about\n- Validate the experience of the sleep issues and the impact of the girlfriend's comment\n- Perhaps gently reflect on the connection they made between the comment and the worsening\n- Maybe ask one clarifying question or offer a gentle reflection\n\nI'll keep it warm, not overly structured, and natural. I won't diagnose or assume too much. I'll reflect back what they said and maybe gently explore the feeling of \"귀찮아지는 느낌\" or the impact of that comment.\n",
